  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA quiet start to the day with surface high pressure sliding\neastward just north of the area, currently centered near Erie,\nPA. This has allowed for mainly clear conditions overnight, with\nvalley fog having already quickly developed across the southern\ncoalfields and into the northern lowlands (southeast of the Ohio\nRiver). While do expect a bit more expansion of fog through \ndawn, weak dry advection via northeast flow should prevent fog \nfrom getting too widespread across our northern zones, and could\neven begin mixing it out in some areas. Patchy dense fog will \nhowever remain possible.\n\nHigh pressure quickly slides northeast of the area throughout\ntoday, with return flow and associated moisture advection \nbringing the chance for isolated to widely scattered showers and\na few storms in and near the higher terrain during the \nafternoon and evening hours, as a cutoff upper disturbance \nacross the southeast CONUS gradually shifts north towards the \nforecast area. An isolated shower could occur as far north as \nthe Charleston metro. Given very weak steering flow, generally \n5-10 kts, a highly isolated instance of flash flooding cannot \nentirely be ruled out across our southwest VA and far southern \nWV zones if thunderstorms were to develop given PWATs quickly \nreturning to 1.5\"+ by later today, and also considering the \npockets of very heavy rain that occurred Sunday evening in some \nof these areas. Activity should steadily diminish near/after \nsunset with the loss of daytime heating. Some river valley fog \nis expected tonight, and perhaps even a bit of drizzle across \nthe eastern slopes of the mountains within low stratus courtesy \nof southeast upslope flow.\n\nAfter a refreshing morning with lows in the mid 50s to mid 60s,\nhigh temperatures today will warm into the mid/upper 80s across\nthe lowlands. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe aforementioned cutoff upper disturbance will gradually be\npulled northward across the area Tuesday night and Wednesday as\na broad upper trough across the north-central CONUS gradually \nprogesses eastward. This combined with continued return flow and \nincreased warmth/moisture across the area via an Atlantic/Gulf\nfeed will result in increasing shower and storm chances \nbeginning Wednesday onward, with the best chance for rain being \nduring the afternoon/evening hours with diurnal heating (PoPs \ngenerally 40-80%). The pattern remains active Thursday through \nthe weekend courtesy of a fairly zonal/progressive upper air \npattern with weak crossing waves that will result in continued \ndaily shower and storm chances. The potential for organized \nsevere weather during this timeframe continues to look on the \nlower side, as the stronger kinematics generally remaining \nnorth of the area. However, daily modest instability/forcing \nwill support at least some strong storm potential, with progged \nsoundings during the period generally favoring weak shear amid a\nvery moist environment, which would trend towards a damaging \nwind threat via wet microbursts if stronger storms were to \noccur. In addition, PWATs in the 1.5-2.0\" range amid continued \ngeneral weak steering flow will result in a localized threat for\nflash flooding with diurnally driven storms.\n\nTemperatures are progged to be slightly above normal throughout\nthe period, with highs generally in the mid to upper 80s across\nthe lowlands, amid high humidity.",
